|~ The module of the command line, a noun of the core, `::qlang/cli`,
   written short `::cli`: its verbs live on it, and their
   primitives, in `cli/src/io-operands.mjs` and `format-operands.mjs`,
   are plain functions over the values the head of
   each verb checks; the refusals of a verb sit under it. ~|

::qlang/cli
  |~~ The command line of qlang: `@in`, `@out`, `@err` and `@tap` read
      and write the streams of the process, and `table` renders a
      vector of maps as text. A value one query hands another over a
      pipe keeps its kinds as its literal: `print | @out` writes it and
      `@in | parse | open` reads it back.
      A query runs on what stdin holds: JSON read as values, an object
      a map whose keys are keywords, whose answer is written as JSON,
      where a value JSON has no form for is refused; text read
      as a string, whose answer is written as its `print`; and
      `| json` writes JSON text. Every query the command line
      runs calls its verbs by their names, and `::cli | manifest` lists
      them.
  ~~|

::AnswerNotJsonError
  |~~ The input was JSON, so the answer is written as JSON, and a value
      under it has no form there: a quote, a keyword, a tag or a value
      under a tag; a set is written as the array of its elements.
      `:path` holds the map keys and the indices to it, `:actualType` its
      kind. The query ends before the answer is data: `* (…)` runs a body
      where `* ~(…)` hands each element the quote, `| keyword` answers the
      name of a keyword, and `payload` a value without its tag. ~~|

:@in
  |~~ Reads stdin to completion and returns it as a String,
      ignoring the incoming pipeValue. A TTY-attached stdin (no
      piped input) yields the empty string immediately so a
      prompt-less `qlang '@in | ...'` does not hang. Producer —
      use as the head of a pipeline.

      Pattern: `echo '{"x":42}' | qlang '@in | parseJson | /x'`
      reads the JSON payload, parses it, and projects `:x`. ~~|
  ::verb~(:subject ::any | :returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/cli/@in})

:@out
  |~~ Writes a String to stdout followed by `\n` and passes the
      original pipeValue through unchanged on the success track.
      Bare, it writes the subject, a String; given a text, a
      renderer computed against the subject, it writes that. The
      pipeValue itself stays put either way — `@out` is identity on
      the success track regardless of what bytes left the channel.

      Pattern: `"hello" | @out` writes "hello\n", value continues
      as "hello". With a renderer: `42 | @out json` writes "42\n",
      value continues as 42 — the rest of the pipeline still sees
      the number, not the string. ~~|
  ::verb~(:subject ::any | :text #[::string ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/cli/@out})

::OutSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Bare `@out` requires a String subject — the bytes
      written to stdout must already be a String. Wrap a
      non-String value in a renderer:
      `42 | @out json` or `{:x 1} | @out print`. ~~|

::OutRendererResultNotStringError
  |~~ `@out renderer` requires the renderer to produce a
      String. A renderer that returns a Number / Map / null
      cannot be written to stdout — wrap it through `json` or
      `print`. ~~|

:@err
  |~~ Same shape as `@out` but writes to stderr — the diagnostic
      channel. Use for log lines, traces, progress indicators
      that should not enter the program's data output.

      Pattern: `[1 2 3] | @err "counting" | count` writes
      "counting" to stderr and answers 3 on stdout. ~~|
  ::verb~(:subject ::any | :text #[::string ::null] | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/cli/@err})

::ErrSubjectNotStringError
  |~~ Bare `@err` requires a String subject. Wrap non-String
      values through a renderer, `json` or `print`. ~~|

::ErrRendererResultNotStringError
  |~~ `@err renderer` requires the renderer to produce a
      String. ~~|

:@tap
  |~~ Passes its subject on and writes
      `[tap <label>] <the literal of the pipeValue>\n` to stderr,
      keyword label included verbatim for grep'ability. Use
      to peek at intermediate values without altering the
      pipeline shape.

      Pattern: `data | @tap :before | filter ~(active) | @tap :after | count`
      prints both pre- and post-filter snapshots to stderr,
      then returns the count. ~~|
  ::verb~(:subject ::any | :label ::keyword | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/cli/@tap})

::TapLabelNotKeywordError
  |~~ `@tap` label must be a Keyword — use `:foo` not `"foo"`
      so the prefix renders as `[tap foo]` consistent with the
      keyword's grammatical form. ~~|


:table
  |~~ Renders a Vec of Maps as a fixed-width tabular string, a view
      for a terminal. Columns are derived from keys in first-occurrence
      order across all rows; missing keys render as empty cells. A
      String cell prints bare, null as an empty cell, and every other
      value as its literal on one line. Empty Vec yields the marker
      "(empty)".

      ~([{:name "alice" :age 30} {:name "bob" :age 25}] | table | contains "alice")
      ~([{:at {:file "A.java" :line 12}}] | table | contains "{:file \"A.java\" :line 12}")
      ~([] | table | eq "(empty)")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:subject ::vec | :returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/cli/table})

::TableSubjectNotVecError
  |~~ Subject of `table` must be Vec.
      ~(
        42 | table
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TableSubjectNotVecError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::TableRowNotMapError
  |~~ Element of `table` input collection must be Map.
      ~(
        [42 "hello"] | table
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::TableRowNotMapError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|
